{:edb/db-before-t 2654 :edb/db-after-t 2655 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535967 50 #inst "2026-10-15T13:08:22.271Z" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550122 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550125 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550126 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550139 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550150 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550151 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550154 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1038 #edb/ref 17592186550166 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186092219 1040 #edb/ref 17592186550168 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186550156 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186550158 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550122 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{3})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550122 1072 #uuid "09b619c0-b536-5243-a608-187bb7fdbae4" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550122 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{\\pi}{3})=(2,\\frac{\\pi}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi}{3}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550123 1080 "(2,6)" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550123 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550123 1083 #uuid "0b8f1b37-d179-55a2-8bdb-b8e5114e6a07" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550124 1080 "1" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550124 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550124 1083 #uuid "0bbdaaf9-dc0c-5d8d-8232-0e7af48e54c6" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186550129 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186550135 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550125 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550125 1072 #uuid "160d6d55-a68a-5cd0-a8b9-c87c2fd8df95" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550125 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begi