rue] [17592186550150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550150 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(\\sqrt{8},\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(16,\\theta +\\pi ).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-2,-2).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550150 1072 #uuid "73990ad1-cfa6-5a8e-93b3-fc46b89fc10a" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550150 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-2}{-2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(16,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+\\pi )=(16,\\frac{9\\pi }{4})=(16,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(8\\sqrt{2},8\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186550149 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186550163 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550151 1071 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,0)$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(2r,\\frac{5\\pi}{6})$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(1,0)$.\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550151 1072 #uuid "7423cb94-dc90-5a23-868c-cd90a0dda4e8" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550151 1074 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(0)^{2}} \\\\   & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(2\\cdot(1),\\frac{5\\pi}{6})=(2,\\frac{5\\pi}{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\   & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\   & =2\\cdot\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi}{6}) \\\\   & =2\\cdot(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\   & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},1).$" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550152 1080 "(4\\sqrt{2},4\\sqrt{2})" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550152 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550152 1083 #uuid "74fafcb0-63e1-558c-b040-1dbfd5369cba" 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550123 13194139535967 17592186045443 true] [17592186550153 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550136 13194139535967 175921